From: pdx203 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Subject: scanout: copy four pixels a thread; time the copy and the frame on the GPU

The BGRA scanout copy (scanout_opaque, one 4-byte load and store a thread) took 5.1 ms of GPU a
frame for 2 x 11 MB (1096x2560), measured with the runtime's GPU timestamps. scanout_opaque4 does
16-byte loads and stores and takes 0.83 ms. It is used wherever both pitches and the width are
multiples of 16 bytes, once its first frame matched the surface (else the one-pixel kernel stays).

A "PG" report row every 120 frames gives the copy's GPU time, commit-to-scheduled and
scheduled-to-completed, and the GPU time of the process's command buffers (full-screen passes and
the rest, NBGpuTimes). PF rows are thinned once PG runs: the kernel shows only the newest line.

